u  know as long as I'm at that level that  mean  when I'm listening there's things that I  don't get when I read there's things  that I don't get I want to get it down  below 10% so when I've learned so many  words that I can pick up a piece of  Russian that capture or a news article  or whatever and and I have less than 10%  unknown words then I think I have  achieved the level where I can start  focusing on output more focusing on  grammar you know instead of reading for  meaning or listening for meaning and  noticing and saving words for meaning  I'll start saving phrases that contain  you know the different case changes and  other things that happen in Russian and  at that time I will do more talking and  and and and writing so you know what is  the role of output at an earlier stage I  believe it is still important at an  early stage not so much as a certainly  insofar as speaking is concerned not so  much as a as a opportunity to learn the  language but it serves other purposes  first of all it helps to keep you  motivated if you know you have to meet a  teacher like here at at link to you  having a an appointment with your tutor  next Tuesday or every Tuesday or twice a  week that helps to motivate you you  don't want to disappoint the tutor when  you are speaking you sort of come across  some of the things that you have trouble  saying so then you start to look for  those when you're listening and reading  so it forces you to prepare a little bit  I mean I believe that the writing is  more effective than speaking insofar as  finding problem areas in your language  because you have a record and it's  easier from a written text specially the  way we do it or we correct the writing  and we give you correct phrases and then  you can import this into link and you  can save the words that you didn't use  correctly and treat that as as learning  content so the writing is pretty  important but also is the speaking even  though when you direct people when they  speak they tend to repeat the same error  over and over again it's it's  nevertheless it's an exercise it's a  challenge it shouldn't be too much done  too much and the big thing is one  shouldn't think or hope that  just having conversations is going to  teach you the language for a variety of  reasons first of all it's very difficult  to have a conversation just for the sake  of having a conversation with someone  that you aren't necessarily interested  in having a conversation with so okay  what did you do on the weekend what do  you think the global warming you know  when your once you've gone through a few  of these there's no much stuff to talk  about if you don't have a genuine  interest in communicating or in having a  conversation and it's made all the more  difficult because you're doing in a  foreign language so a little bit of  talking once or twice a week writing  once or twice a week a major focus on  input until you reach a stage where your  comprehension both listening  comprehension and your ability to enjoy  reading in a language have become so  strong that now you are ready to take  what you have built up and move into the  output and and I believe that at that  time if you if you've emphasized the  input and the listening and the reading  you'll have a strong vocabulary you'll  be more confident you'll have better  pronunciation then if you were to do it  the traditional way by the traditional  right way I mean trying to remember  everything that's in Chapter one trying  to say it right trying to do the  exercises then once you've mastered  Chapter one you go to chapter two or  sitting in a classroom with 20 people  all of whom speak the language that  you're studying very poorly so that what  you are immersed in is not the language  as spoken by native speakers with real  meaning and real content but a lot of  people stumbling like you which I think  is is a very very low intensity language  environment so to sum up output is  important even at an early stage even  though from a learner's perspective the  emphasis initially should be on  understanding reading and listening and  building up vocabulary and getting used  to the language but even in that early  stage as soon as you feel up to it once  or twice a week to challenge yourself  initially one-on-one because you don't  speak well enough to sit in a group you  would just annoy them and embarrass  yourself and eventually and we notices  with our link learners after six months  or so  one then getting a group three or four  on one and do that once or twice a week  and also take the time to write because  that's where you really pick up on where  your structural problems are and and  that helps you them to look for those  kinds of words and phrases and  structures in the language as you're  listening and reading so a very quick  discussion then on the place for output  and and I guess the key thing there is  to not feel inadequate because you  aren't able to say something early and I  don't agree with with the people who say  that you only need 800 words and you can  be fluent or no you can't or if you if  you master five phrases to use at the  bank and another five phrases to use at  the restaurant that you'll be ok yeah  that might work if your goal is to go  down to Mexico and hope to be able to  say buenos dias to a few people but if  you want to learn a language that just  is not going to do it so I believe in  that initial input phase silent phase  then followed by a much stronger output  phase and at that point you'll feel much  more confident with your output now I  have to put my glasses on again to see  how to turn this thing off and thank you  very much for listening
 

